course: arts
lesson: 14
title: Painting: paints, brushes, mess and mistakes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Different paints behave differently: watercolour is thin and spreads in wet paper, acrylic is plastic-based and dries in minutes, and oil paint dries slowly so painters can blend it smoothly for days.
explain: Different brushes do different jobs: big flat ones for skies and backgrounds, small round ones for details, so painters usually start big and finish small.
explain: Picture Hannah's watercolour sky bleeding into the sea: she panics, but that spreading is often the most beautiful bit, and many painters say "happy accidents" (a phrase made famous by the TV painter Bob Ross) give them ideas.
explain: The other half of painting is knowing when to stop, asking before you paint on walls, and dressing for mess, because dried acrylic will not wash out of your school jumper.
explain: People say it their way: "trash" means rubbish, "it's a vibe" is a joke for "interesting", and "kiddo" is a friendly word for a child.
fact: Big flat brushes suit skies and backgrounds; small round brushes with a pointed tip suit details.
fact: Watercolour is thin paint mixed with water; painting on wet paper so colours spread is called wet-on-wet, and waiting for paint to dry stops the bleeding.
fact: Watercolour can sometimes be lifted with a tissue, and spills can be turned into clouds or water.
fact: Oil paint is pigment mixed with oil, often linseed oil; it dries slowly, so painters could blend smoothly.
fact: A clear varnish protects an oil painting, but it yellows over time; restorers, experts who carefully clean old art, can reveal bright colours under old varnish and dirt.
fact: A mural is a big planned painting on a wall; without permission painting on walls is damage, even at home.
fact: Acrylic paint dries in minutes and cleans with water while wet; it is the usual paint in schools, and many famous modern artists used it.
fact: Very thick paint that sticks out from the canvas is called impasto; it can be done with oil paint or with acrylic, which dries much faster.
fact: Loose brushwork, holding the brush further from the bristles, often makes paintings look more alive.
fact: Pysanky are Ukrainian Easter eggs decorated with wax and dye, with patterns that carry meanings like the sun, wheat or waves; there is a pysanka museum in Kolomyia shaped like a giant egg.
fact: Overworking means adding too much until the picture gets muddy; painters step back and look from a distance.
fact: Acrylic becomes plastic when dry and does not wash out easily, so artists wear old clothes or aprons.
exercise: Noah's tiny windows turn into blobs with his big brush. What should he use? | A small round brush for details | An even bigger brush, because big brushes are always neater | His fingers instead of any brush | The same big brush, pressed much harder onto the paper
exercise: Which brush suits a big sky? | A big flat brush | A tiny round brush | A toothbrush | A pencil
exercise: Hannah's watercolours bleed into each other. What is true? | Bleeding is often the best effect, and waiting for dry paint stops it | It is always a mistake that ruins the whole picture, every time | Watercolours should never touch wet paper | She must start again from scratch with oil paint, which never spreads at all
exercise: What is wet-on-wet? | Painting on wet paper so colours spread | Painting outside in the rain on purpose | Washing brushes between colours | Varnishing a wet painting
exercise: Jayden spills water over his painting. What is wise? | Stay calm, dab it and see what he can make of it | Bin it at once, because a spill always ruins work | Pour on more water to even it out | Rub it hard with his sleeve until the water is all gone
exercise: Why do old oil paintings still look shiny? | Oil paint and a clear protective varnish | They are repainted by the museum every year | They are covered in plastic film | Old paint never ages or fades at all, whatever happens
exercise: What do restorers do? | Carefully clean and care for old art | Paint new copies over the old paintings | Sell paintings in shops | Guard the museums at night with torches
exercise: Dmytro paints his bedroom wall without asking. What is true? | Without permission it is damage, even at home | If it is art, it never matters where it goes or who owns it | Bedroom walls belong to children | Murals never need anyone's permission, even on other people's walls
exercise: Charlotte wants thick blobs of paint. What can do the job? | Acrylics, which dry faster than oils | Only oil paint, as it's the only paint real artists use | Watercolour, which is the thickest paint of all | Felt-tip pens pressed very hard onto paper
exercise: What is impasto? | Very thick paint that sticks out from the canvas | Paint so thin you can see right through it to the paper | A kind of Italian brush | A clear shiny varnish put on at the very end
exercise: Mohammed's perfect lines look stiff. What should he try? | Painting more loosely, letting his arm move | Using a ruler for every single line he paints | Painting even more slowly and tightly than before | Pressing the brush much harder to make each line bolder
exercise: Emma asks if Sasha's pysanka patterns are doodles. What are they? | Old Ukrainian symbols with meanings, like sun or wheat | Random doodles with no meaning, drawn just to fill space | Modern logos copied from shops | Patterns invented last year for a school art project in Kyiv
exercise: Where is a pysanka museum shaped like a giant egg? | Kolomyia | London | Paris | Tokyo
exercise: Freddie keeps adding paint until it is a dark mess. What is that called? | Overworking | Wet-on-wet | Impasto | Varnishing
exercise: Josh says aprons are for babies, then gets paint on his jumper. What is true? | Artists wear aprons, and dried acrylic doesn't wash out | Aprons are only for little kids, never for real artists | All paint washes out easily | Paint never stains clothes once it has dried on them properly
test: Your mate goes, "one brush is enough for everything, innit." What's the problem? | Different brushes suit big areas and small details | One brush is exactly what all real painters use for everything | More brushes make the paint dry slower | Brushes are not needed for painting
test: Jayden shouts "Oh crap, it's wrecked!" after the spill. What would a calm painter do? | Dab it and maybe turn the stain into clouds | Throw the painting in the bin and start a new one | Spill more water to hide it | Blame the teacher for putting the water pot so close
test: What would happen to an old painting cleaned by a restorer who removes dirt and yellow varnish? | Brighter colours could show again | It would turn completely black within a few days | The paint would fall off the canvas | Nothing would ever change, however long it was
test: Dmytro's mum says "Well that's a vibe, but you're repainting it Saturday, mister." What does she mean? | It's interesting, but he must cover it up | She loves it and wants more walls painted the same | He must paint the whole house | The wall will dry by Saturday, so he can paint more then
test: A cartoon artist uses only oil paint "because real artists never use anything else". What is wrong? | Many famous modern artists used acrylics | Nothing, oil is the only real paint there is | Real artists only use crayons | Oil paint dries in seconds, so artists have to rush
test: Freddie's aunt says, "Knowing when to stop is half the job, kiddo." What should he do while painting? | Step back often and stop when it looks right | Keep adding more paint until the paper is full | Never stop until the whole picture is completely dark | Paint with his eyes shut so he does not see the mess
test: Your teacher says a picture should always be as neat as possible. What could be added? | Some styles look better loose and free | Neatness is the only thing that matters in all art | Messy pictures are banned in all galleries | Loose painting is only for babies who can't hold a brush
test: What would happen if you painted watercolour onto a still-wet patch? | The new colour would spread into the wet part | The paint would sit perfectly still where it was put | The paper would turn to stone | The colour would vanish
test: Emma calls pysanka patterns "just doodles". What would be a good reply? | Many folk patterns carry old meanings and stories | She's right, folk art never means anything | Pysanky are only coloured with felt tips | Pysanky are a kind of pizza
test: Noah says his brush is "trash" (rubbish). What is the likely real problem? | He is using a big brush for tiny details | His brush is made of rubbish | All brushes are the same | He needs an even bigger brush for the tiny windows
test: Why do painters step back from their work? | To see the whole picture from a distance | To rest their tired legs | To let the paint dry faster | To hide from the teacher
exam: Identify the principal characteristic of oil paint by virtue of which painters could achieve smooth blending. | It dries slowly, permitting prolonged working of the paint | It dries within a matter of seconds upon contact with the air | It is diluted exclusively with water | It is incapable of being blended
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the painting of a mural upon a wall. | Permission is required, and in its absence such painting constitutes damage | No permission is required for any work of art upon any wall whatsoever | Permission is required only for walls in public buildings | Murals may be painted solely by children
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "overworking"? | The addition of excessive paint or detail until the picture becomes muddied | The working of a painter beyond the hours of the art room | The application of a protective varnish to a painting | The painting upon wet paper so that colours spread
exam: Ascertain the reason for which artists wear aprons when working with acrylic paint. | Dried acrylic becomes plastic and is not readily removed from clothing | Aprons are required solely for young children and never for adult artists | Acrylic paint is harmless to every fabric | Aprons improve the quality of the brushwork
